The rhythmic clatter of the rails was Elias Thorne’s heartbeat. For forty-two years, he’d been a conductor on the Blue Ridge Line, a winding route that snaked through the Appalachian Mountains. He wasn't just a conductor; he was a guardian of the rails, a storyteller, and a silent observer of the lives that passed through his care. 

Elias wasn't a man of many words. His face, weathered like the old oak trees lining the tracks, held a quiet dignity. His hands, calloused and strong, moved with practiced ease as he checked tickets, announced stops, and ensured the safety of his passengers. He wore the same uniform every day: a crisp navy blue jacket, a neatly pressed shirt, and a worn, but polished, conductor's hat. It was a uniform of responsibility, a uniform of tradition.

He knew the Blue Ridge Line like the back of his hand. He could identify every bend in the track, every rustle of the wind through the pines, every shift in the mountain air. He knew the stories the landscape held too - the ghost town of Harmony Creek, abandoned after the coal mine dried up; the lovers' leap, where a young couple had tragically ended their lives;
